=== layout: section

# Aneuploidy of Sex Chromosomes

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

What would be the sex of an XXY human? And why?

- A) male
- B) female
- C) cannot be determined

@answer

[Answer\: A]{color=red}

=== layout: one-panel

# *Aneuploidy of Sex Chromosomes*

@body

- Nondisjunction of sex chromosomes produces a variety of aneuploid conditions
- XXX females are healthy, with no unusual physical features
- [*Klinefelter syndrome*]{underline=true} is the result of an extra chromosome in a male, producing XXY individuals
- Monosomy X, called [*Turner syndrome,*]{underline=true} produces X0 females, who are sterile; it is the only known viable monosomy in humans

=== layout: two-panels

# *Female Sex Abnormalities &#8211; 45,X0*

@left

![Slide image 16](assets/lect11c-sex_dosage/image_016.png)

@right

- [Turner's syndrome]{underline=true} &#8211; occurs when females inherit only one X chromosome &#8211; their genotype is X0
  - short in stature, adult average 4' 7&#8221;
  - often have distinctive webbed necks (i.e., extra folds of skin), small jaws, and high arched palates.
  - lack prominent female secondary sexual characteristics.
    - exceptionally small, widely spaced breasts, broad shield-shaped chests, and turned-out elbows.
    - ovaries do not develop normally and [are sterile.]{underline=true}
  - between 1 in 2,000 to 1 in 5,000 female births

=== layout: one-panel

# *Female Sex Abnormalities &#8211; 47,XXX*

@body

- [Triple-X syndrome]{underline=true} &#8211; occurs in women who inherit three X chromosomes &#8211; their genotype is XXX (or more rarely XXXX or XXXXX).
  - As adults, these "super-females" generally are an inch or so taller than average with unusually long legs and slender torsos, but [otherwise appear normal.]{underline=true}
  - They tend to be emotionally immature for their size during childhood.  This sometimes results in teachers and other adults labeling them as troublemakers.
  - 1 in 1,000 female births
  - Approximately 100 cases of XXXX have been reported worldwide
  - Approximately 25 females have been diagnosed with XXXXX syndrome.

=== layout: two-panels

# *Male Sex Abnormalities &#8211; 47,XXY*

@left

![Slide image 17](assets/lect11c-sex_dosage/image_017.png)

@right

- [Klinefelter syndrome]{underline=true} &#8211; males inherit one or more extra X chromosomes &#8211; their genotype is XXY (or more rarely XXXY or XY/XXY mosaic).
  - high-pitched voices
  - asexual to feminine body contours
    - as well as breast enlargement, and comparatively little facial and body hair.
  - [sterile or nearly so]{underline=true}
    - testes and prostate gland are small.
  - Slightly above average height and likely to be overweight.
  - between 1 in 500 and 1 in 1000 male births

=== layout: one-panel

# *Male Sex Abnormalities &#8211; 47,XYY*

@body

- XYY syndrome &#8211; males inherit an extra Y chromosome &#8211; their genotype is XYY.
  - As adults, these "super-males" are usually tall (above 6 feet) and generally appear and act normal.
    - However, they produce high levels of testosterone.
    - During adolescence, they often are slender, have severe facial acne, and are poorly coordinated.
  - They are usually fertile and lead ordinary lives as adults.
    - Many, if not most, are unaware that they have a chromosomal abnormality.
  - Between 1 in 900 and 1 in 2,000 male births

=== layout: two-panels

# *Richard Speck*

@left

- an American mass murderer who systematically tortured, raped, and murdered 8 nurses from South Chicago Community Hospital in 1966
- a Swiss endocrinologist suggested, based on Jacobs' unsubstantiated theory and Speck's height and acne that Speck might have XYY syndrome
  - It was really taken up by the popular press

@right

![Slide image 18](assets/lect11c-sex_dosage/image_018.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# *XYY and Violent Crimes*

@body

- In 1965 and 1966, British cytogeneticist Patricia Jacobs' [hypothesis]{color=red}, that men with XYY syndrome are more prone to aggressive and violent behavior than males with the normal XY karyotype
- [Speculation]{color=red} even ran that Richard Speck had natural propensity to violence to because he had an extra Y chromosome.
  - a Swiss endocrinologist [suggested]{color=red}, based on Jacobs' [unsubstantiated]{color=red} theory and Speck's height that Speck [might have]{color=red} XYY syndrome
  - analysis [revealed]{color=red} that Speck had a [normal]{color=red} XY karyotype
  - fake news
- In the mid-1970s, a Danish study showed that XYY men were [not more likely]{color=red} to commit violent crimes
  - bias problems with original data were because they looked only a criminals and not the general population

=== layout: one-panel

# *Male Sex Abnormalities &#8211; 48, XXYY*

@body

- XXYY syndrome &#8211; sex chromosome anomaly in which males have an extra X and Y chromosome.
  - The appearance of at least one Y chromosome with a properly functioning SRY gene makes a male. Therefore, humans with XXYY are normally males.
  - Mostly similar to 47, XYY super males.
    - additional learning disabilities and medical problems
  - one in every 18,000&#8211;40,000 male births

